10. Epic 1 User Stories
Story 1 — Model Selection
As a system architect,
I want to evaluate available Generative AI models,
so that LegalEase uses an appropriate model for legal-document generation.

Acceptance Criteria:

Gemini models are evaluated.

Context length is considered.

Reasoning capability is considered.

Structured-output support is considered.

Cost/performance considerations are documented.

Gemini 2.5 Pro is selected for the initial implementation.

Story 2 — Application Architecture
As a developer,
I want a modular LegalEase architecture,
so that frontend, backend, AI, database, and document-generation components can be developed independently.

Acceptance Criteria:

Frontend communicates through backend APIs.

AI calls are isolated in an AI service.

Database access is separated from business logic.

Document generation is a separate service/module.

API keys are not exposed to the client.

Story 3 — AI Integration
As a user,
I want my legal requirements to be converted into a structured document,
so that I can review and download the generated document.

Acceptance Criteria:

User requirements are validated.

A structured prompt is created.

Gemini generates the document content.

AI output conforms to the expected schema.

Invalid output is detected.

Valid output can be converted to DOCX/PDF.

11. Final Architecture Decision
LegalEase will use a layered architecture with Gemini 2.5 Pro as the Generative AI service.

The architecture separates:

User Interface → Backend API → Prompt/AI Service → Gemini 2.5 Pro → Validation → Document Generation → Storage

This design provides modularity, scalability, security, structured AI output, and easier maintenance, while keeping document formatting and application logic under the control of LegalEase rather than relying entirely on the AI model.
